WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.310 --> 00:00:04.831
This is special effects
legend Kevin Yagher.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.507
And here, he's adding some color

00:00:09.507 --> 00:00:12.174
to the nipple of this fake bodysuit.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.240
But as he moves the airbrush up,

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.050
he's adding a much more subtle change.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.970
You can see the difference
between the bottom of the suit

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.960
and the top part that
he hasn't gotten to yet.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.740
What this spray paint does
is give the suit texture,

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.750
mimicking the undertones
human skin naturally has

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:36.700
and giving the appearance
of tiny details like pores.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:40.650
From a life cast picking up on
an actor's defining features

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.667
to sculpting subtle veins

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:45.000
to mastering a jiggly stomach,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.940
a lot of effort goes into making

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.440
the bodysuits actors wear look realistic.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.700
Warning: Some spoilers ahead

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.907
for "Bill &amp; Ted Face the Music."

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.870
Bodysuits let actors go through
physical transformations

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.240
that they couldn't
easily make on their own,

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:08.280
from gaining weight to gaining
muscle to being pregnant.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.930
There are times when bodysuits
don't need to be as detailed

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.700
because they're covered in clothing,

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.200
like this suit Kevin Yagher
designed for Dana Carvey

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.200
in an ultimately cut sequence
from "The Master of Disguise."

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.190
But because Keanu Reeves and Alex Winter

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:27.070
appear shirtless in "Bill
&amp; Ted Face the Music,"

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:30.138
both the muscle suits and fat bodysuit

00:01:30.138 --> 00:01:31.946
created for scenes where they meet

00:01:31.946 --> 00:01:34.029
future versions of themselves

00:01:34.029 --> 00:01:36.580
had to be incredibly detailed.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:39.153
To make a bodysuit, you first have to get

00:01:39.153 --> 00:01:42.228
a life cast of an actor's entire torso.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.260
Narrator: Keanu and Alex were covered

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.950
in purple and green silicone rubbers.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.350
The green silicone has an oil in it

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:54.700
that ensures it won't pull
any of the actors' hairs

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:55.710
out of their bodies.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.000
The two silicones adhere to each other

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.910
and are then wrapped in a
temporary plaster jacket

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:01.950
to hold them together,

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.630
which will eventually be
replaced by a fiberglass shell.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:09.000
That forms the mold that
will shape the bodysuits.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.770
The fiberglass torso shell is laid up

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:13.370
into the silicone impression,

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.210
which then gets covered in clay

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.380
for the sculpting part of the process.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.820
This is the first stage where attention

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:22.510
to every realistic detail is crucial,

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:26.370
as the team sculpts every
muscle and vein onto this mold.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:27.997
Kevin Yagher: I just wanted
to make sure it looked,

00:02:27.997 --> 00:02:30.750
at every angle, like if he
lifted his arm or something,

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.740
that everything was finished
beautifully down here

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.060
just as much as it was up here.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.640
Narrator: In early design
and Photoshop stages,

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.780
Alex said he actually
wanted the muscular Bill

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.480
to look like a gorilla.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.000
Even if the suit's shape is over the top,

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.043
the basic anatomy can still be convincing.

00:02:47.043 --> 00:02:50.530
Sculptor Carol Koch kept a
model of the human anatomy

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:52.420
in front of her as a reference

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.750
while chipping away at the muscle suits.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.441
Kevin says a common misconception is that

00:02:57.441 --> 00:03:00.374
when muscles bulge, the veins will too,

00:03:00.374 --> 00:03:03.440
but veins actually don't get that big.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.960
Kevin: I would have them just keep making

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:06.830
those veins subtle, more subtle.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.210
So they would just keep smoothing them out

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:09.420
until I could barely see them,

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.480
where they're there, but
they didn't look, you know,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:13.810
like if they were worms.

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:14.870
And then when I come back with a painting,

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:16.472
that also pops them up,

00:03:16.472 --> 00:03:20.060
so the combination of a
nice sculpture with paint

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.770
is what makes them look real.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:23.410
Narrator: Once they've sculpted the shape

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:26.510
of the bodysuits with all
their details in clay,

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:30.340
they make a mold of the clay
suit using a thicker silicone,

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:32.900
combine the life cast torso shell,

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.633
and then inject foam latex into it.

00:03:35.633 --> 00:03:39.040
The outer layer of foam
is removed and replaced

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.490
with a skin-colored layer of silicone.

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.430
What about silicone makes
it the right material

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:45.730
for human skin?

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.070
Well, foam wrinkles easily,

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:50.650
and silicone can soften those wrinkles.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.613
But more importantly, Kevin says,

00:03:52.613 --> 00:03:56.552
silicone absorbs light the
same way human skin does,

00:03:56.552 --> 00:04:00.830
which goes a long way towards
making the suits look real.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:03.500
But it isn't until
after the suit is sealed

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.970
and injected with yet more silicone

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:08.960
that Kevin can start to
paint details on the skin,

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.900
as plain foam doesn't look very convincing

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:13.510
as an outer skin layer.

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:16.070
The next thing to nail was skin tone.

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:19.395
When trying to nail the color
palette of the actors' skin,

00:04:19.395 --> 00:04:21.620
Kevin started with lighter shades

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:24.650
to match Keanu and Alex's complexions.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.810
But the prison scene in "Face the Music"

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:29.360
was shot in New Orleans during July,

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.000
when temperatures reached
nearly 100 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.760
And the scene with the muscle suits

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:36.470
was shot in a prison yard,

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.882
so Kevin knew the actors would be spending

00:04:38.882 --> 00:04:40.823
a lot of time in the sun.

00:04:40.823 --> 00:04:43.205
So to compensate, he made the suits

00:04:43.205 --> 00:04:45.863
a shade darker than
their normal skin tone.

00:04:45.863 --> 00:04:47.646
Or, as Kevin says,

00:04:47.646 --> 00:04:49.780
he "gave the suits a suntan."

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:51.690
Which gets to an important point.

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:55.410
Color makes a big difference
in making the skin look real.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.080
Take a look at the muscle suits

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.500
during a nearly completed stage.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.590
The abs pop out not just
because of how they're sculpted,

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:04.230
but because of how they're colored.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:07.230
The team used an unexpected
color combination

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:11.150
of red and green paint
underneath and around each ab.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:14.800
It's those kinds of surprising
details in human skin

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.893
that only an artist can detect.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.730
Let's go back into that
blank area of the muscle suit

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:22.570
he colored in with an airbrush.

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:26.180
Kevin says there's always
some red in a person's skin.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.400
So he starts with that red base

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:30.910
and then adds shades of green and blue,

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:33.070
other colors he's observed in the skin.

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:35.410
Kevin: I use a very
tiny, tiny little spray.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:37.593
So, it only maybe puts out, oh,

00:05:37.593 --> 00:05:40.584
a 16th of an inch a band of color.

00:05:40.584 --> 00:05:43.960
I do this thing, for some
reason I call it wobbly hand,

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:47.393
and I just kind of
create a kind of wobble,

00:05:47.393 --> 00:05:49.140
and I also go in and out.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:52.060
So I'm constantly moving
my hand like this,

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:55.390
and then I'm going in and
out, you know, to the thing.

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:56.806
It causes little dots;

00:05:56.806 --> 00:05:59.739
it causes little veins; a little breakup.

00:05:59.739 --> 00:06:01.030
Narrator: While that is happening,

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:03.790
the left nipple doesn't fully pass

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:07.440
until a pinkish-brown
coat gets sprayed on it.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.130
The muscle suits were smooth and hairless

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:14.170
to mimic bodybuilders, but
Keanu's fat suit had hair.

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:17.970
Adding hair to a suit makes it
easier to hide skin details,

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:20.050
but each hair has to look real,

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:22.570
from how it curls to the growth pattern.

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:25.985
Kevin punched at least
300 individual hairs

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:29.390
into the stomach and chest
of Keanu's fake paunch.

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:32.550
He made sure to make the
hair thicker and heavier

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:35.600
as it went down the
chest towards the groin.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:38.710
Kevin's work isn't only
about painting splotches

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:39.890
and punching hairs.

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:43.070
You can't have a bodysuit
that just looks realistic.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:45.850
It also has to move like a real body.

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:49.690
The foam latex beneath the
layer of skin-like silicone

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:51.860
helps the actors move comfortably.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:54.960
A fat suit, like the one
Kevin made for Keanu,

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.290
needs to bounce and jiggle naturally.

00:06:57.290 --> 00:07:00.900
You can see that as Keanu's
stomach pops out of his shirt

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:02.630
during this shot in the film.

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:04.672
So, how do you make it move

00:07:04.672 --> 00:07:06.730
while also giving it some weight?

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:08.163
The classic technique?

00:07:08.163 --> 00:07:10.763
Stuffing the suit with dry lentils.

00:07:10.763 --> 00:07:12.980
Kevin used them when building the suit

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:17.050
for Weird Al Yankovic's
1988 music video "Fat."

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:20.780
He later created two body
pieces to add 40 pounds

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:24.140
to Nicolas Cage in 2002's "Adaptation."

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:27.020
This time around, Kevin
tried to achieve movement

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:29.750
in one of them by mixing lentils and beads

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:32.370
into one of his suits,
but the actor's sweat

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:35.380
actually caused the lentils to sprout.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:38.890
So now, to get the movement
and weight without the mess,

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:42.040
Kevin simply fills the
chest and stomach of a suit

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:43.623
with bags of beads.

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:47.690
Kevin: It kinda jiggles up and down.

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:49.580
Narrator: With all the skill involved

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:53.510
in making these suits look and
move like real human flesh,

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:55.570
some of the realism is achieved

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:58.240
by cleverly hiding the dead giveaways.

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.980
Makeup artists typically
cover up the zippers

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:02.550
on the back of bodysuits,

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.140
but because of that
brutal New Orleans heat,

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:08.070
they kept the zippers on
the muscle suits exposed

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:10.315
so the actors could hop in and out of them

00:08:10.315 --> 00:08:11.740
quickly to cool off.

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:15.940
So for a shot like this, the
zippers were just CGI'd out.

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:18.280
Kevin also needed to hide the seams

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:20.450
where the suit connects to real skin

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:22.580
around the neck and the arms.

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:24.570
So when wearing the muscle suits,

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:27.000
the two actors wore weight-lifting gloves,

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:30.440
while Keanu's long fake
beard hid the neck seam.

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:34.110
You will also notice Keanu
wearing two necklaces

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:36.340
in the shots where he has a bigger belly.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:39.820
The lower one was glued
down to hide the straps

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:42.170
that held the suit up above the chest.

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:44.470
For one final touch of realism,

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:47.130
Kevin applied a water and glycerin mix

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:50.010
to Keanu and Alex's prison bodysuits

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:53.010
to give off a persuasively sweaty look.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:56.100
All that effort, from
punching hundreds of hairs

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:00.380
to wearing a 20- to 25-pound
suit in punishing heat,

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:03.660
created something realistic
not just for the viewer,

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:05.210
but also the actor.

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:07.950
Kevin: Alex Winter liked
to have all that weight,

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.620
because it helped him in his acting

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:13.210
to have enough girth on him
to kind of perform better.

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:15.213
He was happy with that.

